prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a 10×10×10 spring-mass grid (1000 mass points in total) as a 3D soft-body block simulation under gravity and ground collisions.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Place the following importmap in <head> (include it verbatim), and import via ES Modules inside <script type="module">:

<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>

<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
</script>

[Discrete Model]
(1) Grid indices i,j,k ∈ [0,9], with 1000 mass points in total; the initial arrangement is a regular cubic lattice, and the rest distance between adjacent mass points is restLength = 1.0;
(2) Springs:
    - Structural springs: 6-neighbor (±X,±Y,±Z) edges;
    - Diagonal springs: face diagonals and body diagonals within each voxel cell (the implementation must include both body diagonals and face diagonals so that shear is constrained; at minimum, include all within-cell diagonal springs with lengths sqrt(2) and sqrt(3));
(3) Spring force: Hooke's law + damping; stiffness stiffness = 500, damping coefficient damping = 5.0 (linear damping based on velocity difference or relative velocity; units only need to be consistent with the simulation);

[Integration and External Forces]
(4) Update positions using Verlet integration;
(5) Gravity (0, -9.81, 0);
(6) Ground: plane y=0; when a mass point has y<0, it must collide and rebound (use a reasonable coefficient of restitution between 0 and 1; the rebound must be clearly visible rather than sticking to the ground);

[Boundary Conditions]
(7) Pin the four top corner points: (0,9,0), (9,9,0), (0,9,9), and (9,9,9) correspond to the four top-corner mass points in the grid coordinate system, namely the four corners of the top face (k=9), so that their world positions remain fixed after the entire grid undergoes rigid-body translation (equivalent description: the four corner mass points of the top face are pinned and are not moved by integration);
(8) Release the entire structure from a suspended height of approximately 5 (the initial center-of-mass y-coordinate is approximately 5 above the ground plane y=0);

[Wind]
(9) Provide a button or toggle with id="windBtn"; initially windEnabled=false. When enabled, apply an additional constant force in the +Z direction to the mass points (or use a wind field that varies slightly with position); when disabled, apply no wind force;

[Rendering]
(10) Use InstancedMesh or merged geometry to render the mass-point spheres and/or spring line segments, ensuring that the simulation can be observed interactively; use OrbitControls;

[State Exposure (Required, Updated Every Frame)]
window.__3D_STATE__:
  - nodeCount: 1000
  - springCount: number (actual number of springs)
  - centerOfMass: { x, y, z }
  - maxStretch: number, max(current length/rest length) across all springs
  - groundContacts: number, the count of mass points currently in contact with the ground or undergoing penetration correction during the current frame (the number of mass points with y near 0 that are constrained)
  - windEnabled: boolean
  - simTime: number (seconds)

Output a single HTML file that runs when opened directly in a browser.
